      "message": "\u003e James: This error message could be improved. Also, this probably violates the principle of encapsulation...\n\u003e \n\nI guess you could say: \"Codec was initialized with AOM_CODEC_USE_HIGHBITDEPTH and img is non-high bit-depth, or vice versa\". This is verbose, but aligns it with the code a little better.\n\n\u003e I noticed this issue while debugging a failure caused by passing an `img` parameter of format `AOM_IMG_FMT_I420` to an encoder initialized with `AOM_CODEC_USE_HIGHBITDEPTH` and `g_bit_depth\u003dAOM_BITS_10`.\n\u003e \n\u003e Before:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e /usr/local/google/home/wtc/aom.6/aom/test/encode_test_driver.cc:76: Failure\n\u003e Expected equality of these values:\n\u003e   AOM_CODEC_OK\n\u003e     Which is: 0\n\u003e   res\n\u003e     Which is: 8\n\u003e Invalid parameter\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e After:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e /usr/local/google/home/wtc/aom.6/aom/test/encode_test_driver.cc:76: Failure\n\u003e Expected equality of these values:\n\u003e   AOM_CODEC_OK\n\u003e     Which is: 0\n\u003e   res\n\u003e     Which is: 8\n\u003e Codec is high bit-depth but img is not, or vice versa.\n\u003e ```",
